    clientVision: 'The client, a global financial advisor relocating between New York and Miami, acquired this 42nd-floor corner residence in Brickell seeking a peaceful sanctuary high above the city\'s financial district. Their brief prioritized mental decompression: clean sightlines, tactile organic surfaces, absolute acoustic silence, and the total absence of visual clutter. Because their intense travel schedule allowed zero time for contractor management or furniture deliveries, they mandated an all-inclusive turnkey solution. They required that upon their initial arrival, the home be fully functioning—from curated artwork and bespoke Italian furnishings to custom bed linens, scented candles, and complete culinary amenities ready for immediate enjoyment.',
    designChallenge: 'The apartment was delivered in standard developer white-box condition: sterile drywall, harsh recessed downlights, intrusive structural shear walls, and echoing reflective surfaces that amplified street sirens from below. The spatial layout suffered from narrow corridors and inadequate storage that threatened to compromise the client\'s minimalist lifestyle. Furthermore, high-rise building regulations imposed strict elevator booking windows, noise curfews, and prohibition of core drilling. The design challenge required completely disguising structural columns through integrated architectural joinery, upgrading acoustic performance through high-density wall buffers, and installing bespoke European millwork under demanding logistics without disrupting neighboring residents.',
    approach: 'Paula & Amanda Ambrósio reimagined the apartment as a serene architectural envelope characterized by seamless planes, flush detailing, and warm neutral textures. To resolve visual clutter, we clad perimeter corridors and living walls in custom full-height European white oak paneling that conceals doors, storage closets, audio components, and utility access behind continuous vertical reveals.\n\nIn the main salon, low-profile modular seating upholstered in oyster-toned linen faces the floor-to-ceiling glazing, allowing the glittering skyline to serve as a kinetic backdrop without dominating the interior tranquility. An architectural credenza in fluted oak and honed travertine floats weightlessly against a textured lime plaster wall, concealing multimedia electronics.\n\nThe kitchen was elevated through custom cabinetry re-fronted in matte alabaster lacquer with bronze edge pulls, anchored by an expanded waterfall island in honed Taj Mahal quartzite with concealed breakfast stool niches. We replaced developer-grade surface lighting with a bespoke architectural lighting plan featuring low-glare micro-aperture spots and perimeter coves that wash plaster walls in gentle 2700K warmth.\n\nThe private suite was re-engineered as an acoustic cocoon: walls were treated with padded silk wall panels and motorized dual-layer drapery featuring solar sheer and 100% blackout linings. Every drawer and wardrobe was customized with bespoke Italian joinery, suede dividers, and integrated sensor illumination, providing effortless daily order.',
    materialsNarrative: 'The tactile palette of HOME S was curated to deliver visual quietude and sensory richness within a compact urban footprint. Hand-applied Venetian marmorino plaster with fine marble aggregate brings tactile warmth to drywall surfaces, catching morning light without specular reflection. Millwork was fabricated in northern Italy from wire-brushed European white oak finished in a matte zero-sheen protective seal. Countertops and floating plinths utilize honed Taj Mahal quartzite, prized for its subtle crystalline translucence and impervious resistance to staining. All upholstery and window treatments incorporate natural flax linen, brushed cashmere, and supple nubuck leather.',
