    clientVision: 'A private family commissioning a custom 9,500-square-foot waterfront compound in Boca Raton\'s prestigious Royal Palm Yacht & Country Club sought a refined modern aesthetic balancing soaring architectural drama with tactile, inviting warmth. Active boaters and generous hosts, they envisioned a home suited for multi-generational living, casual dockside gatherings, and elegant formal charity galas. They wished to move away from heavy Mediterranean revival tropes in favor of clean contemporary lines, expansive glass, and organic noble materials. Crucially, they requested that the vast 3,000 square feet of covered outdoor loggias, summer kitchens, and cabanas feel as luxuriously appointed and seamlessly detailed as the interior living salons.',
    designChallenge: 'Managing scale and acoustics across a twenty-four-foot double-height great room was paramount; without careful intervention, the vast glass expanses and stone flooring would create an intolerable echoing chamber. Furthermore, establishing seamless material continuity across indoor and outdoor living zones required solving South Florida\'s extreme climate challenges: exterior stone flags and cabinetry had to withstand intense tropical sun, high humidity, and salt spray without degrading. Structurally, the open-concept layout required integrating substantial structural columns into architectural millwork and designing custom ceiling cassettes that conceal high-capacity air handlers while preserving maximum vertical clearance.',
    approach: 'Paula & Amanda Ambrósio structured the estate\'s architectural narrative around horizontal harmony and organic materiality. In the great room, we grounded the vertical scale by designing exposed architectural white oak ceiling beams with concealed indirect LED channels that cast a warm, flattering glow across the room. Acoustic slatted paneling was integrated into upper wall zones to absorb sound reflections completely.\n\nTo establish seamless flow between the interior great room and exterior waterfront loggia, we specified continuous honed Beaumaniere French limestone slabs that extend past flush threshold pocket doors onto the pool terrace. The outdoor loggia features motorized insect screens and automated solar shades recessed entirely into the ceiling framework.\n\nThe culinary wing features double waterfall islands clad in honed Calacatta Paonazzo marble, paired with custom rift-cut walnut cabinetry and patinated bronze reveals. An architectural wine tasting room with frameless temperature-controlled glass links the kitchen to the formal dining salon, functioning as a glowing jewel box along the main axis.\n\nIn the primary retreat, the bedroom frames direct views of the yacht slip through motorized drapery. The suite includes an outdoor garden shower with private limestone privacy walls, double walk-in dressing rooms with bespoke Italian wardrobes, and a spa bath lined in bookmatched marble.',
