    clientVision: 'The clients, an established New England family with deep roots in Palm Beach society, acquired this Mizner-era courtyard estate on North County Road with the goal of restoring its historic dignity while making it a vibrant, light-filled sanctuary for modern family living. Decades of heavy, unsympathetic renovations had left the interiors dark, compartmentalized, and out of touch with contemporary lifestyle. The owners sought to strip away faux-finish excess in favor of a timeless, fresh aesthetic: luminous white plaster, reclaimed limestone floors, delicate brass accents, and custom millwork that felt as though it had always belonged to the architecture.',
    designChallenge: 'Renovating a historic landmark estate in the Town of Palm Beach requires navigating rigorous review by the Landmarks Preservation Commission (ARCOM). Every exterior alteration, window replacement, and structural modification must meet exacting historical fidelity guidelines. Structurally, the century-old masonry walls had settled unevenly, making level joinery installations complex. Furthermore, modern mechanical infrastructure—including multi-zone high-efficiency HVAC, automated smart lighting, and concealed sound systems—had to be routed discreetly through solid clay-tile walls and pecky cypress ceilings without disturbing original plaster moldings or historic ceiling beams.',
    approach: 'Paula & Amanda Ambrósio approached the estate with deep scholarly respect for Palm Beach architectural history, paired with a fresh, contemporary sensibility. We began by removing non-original interior partitions to reopen original sightlines linking the grand drawing room, the morning loggia, and the central courtyard fountain.\n\nIn the drawing room, original pecky cypress beam ceilings were carefully stripped of dark stain and restored with a soft cerused wax finish that reveals the wood\'s organic texture. The floor was laid in reclaimed 18th-century French Beaumaniere limestone flags with hand-chiseled edges that introduce authentic historic patina underfoot.\n\nThe kitchen was reimagined as a bespoke English-inspired culinary salon. Hand-crafted cabinetry in soft alabaster lacquer is accented with woven brass grilles, hand-stitched leather pull tabs, and unlacquered solid brass hardware that patinates naturally. A monolithic island topped with honed Calacatta marble provides a luminous centerpiece for family cooking.\n\nThroughout the private suites, we blended historic architectural proportion with tailored modern furnishings. Pierre Frey linen wallcoverings and bespoke de Gournay murals bring subtle color and texture to bedroom walls, while modern low-profile upholstery provides deep comfort without overwhelming historic proportions.',
    materialsNarrative: 'The material choices for Ocean Palm Villa prioritize heritage authenticity, historic resonance, and artisanal handcraft. Reclaimed French Beaumaniere limestone flags were sourced from historic provencal farmhouses and calibrated for seamless underfloor heating. Cabinetry and architectural joinery utilize solid American walnut and painted maple with hand-brushed oil finishes. Architectural hardware was hand-cast in England from solid unlacquered brass that will gracefully darken with age. Wall coverings and drapery feature organic Belgian linens, silks by Pierre Frey, and hand-painted scenic murals.',
